2.2 Java interface in Tycho

Our Tycho/Java interface is now fully usable, and being used.
We have ported Sun's tcljava interface to Windows NT and HPUX10.20,
and made our changes to tcljava available at
http://ptolemy.eecs.berkeley.edu/~cxh/ptpub/tcljava.html

Porting tcljava to NT means that we can run Tycho with Java classes
under NT, which will allow more people to use our evolving software
infrastructure.  We feel that the combination of a scripted language
such as Itcl and a faster compiled language such as Java will be a
compelling combination for a wide variety of network-aware applications.

The next step for Tycho/Java under NT is to solve some of the Tcl exec
command issues, perhaps by implementing our own exec command written
in Java.  Java threads provide a suitable multitasking model.


2.3 An Object-Oriented and Scripted Interface to Ptolemy

We are constructing a two-level interface between Tycho and Ptolemy
with the objective of being able to maximally leverage the Ptolemy
software in developing the network-capable Java infrastructure.
The lower-level interface is a set of Tcl procedures implemented
in C++ that expose the Ptolemy kernel functionality to the Tcl
scripting environment.  This low-level interface is not meant
to be used directly, but rather is designed to provide a set of
utilities to a higher-level interface.  The higher-level interface
consists of a set of Itcl classes that mirror the key hierarchical
graph data structures of the Ptolemy kernel.  One or the other of
these interfaces (we are still evaluating this question) will also
be implemented in Java, providing a compatible and modular connection
between Tycho and either the C++-based facilities of Ptolemy or the
new Java infrastructure we will be developing under this project.


2.4 Mutable Systems and Recursion

Mutable models capture dynamically changing application structure.
One of the more interesting forms of mutable dataflow models are recursive,
where a hierarchical block contains within it an instance of itself.
In pictures:

   --------------------------------
   |foo                           |
   |                              |
-->|---> x (Bar) ---> y (foo) --->|---->
   |                              |
   --------------------------------

The key to supporting recursion of this type is to not instantiate
or evaluate the inside instance of "foo" (named "y") until it is
actually required.  I.e., it must be instantiated at run time, much
the way a procedure in a language capable of recursion dynamically
allocates memory on the stack only when a procedure is invoked, rather
than statically allocating memory at compile time.  Visual dataflow
models have not historically been capable of supporting recursion
because of a very static view of implementation.

We have outlined a software architecture that exploits the Ptolemy
"wormhole" concept to implement this.  The basic idea of a wormhole
is that it is a hierarchical block that appears from the outside
to be an atomic block.  In the above example, "y" would be initially
instantiated as a wormhole with a reference to the master "foo",
much the way a compiler in a language supporting recursion puts
a reference to common source code at each call of a procedure.
The objects (analogous to memory for automatic variables) required
to execute "y" do not get created until "y" actually fires (analogous
to a function call).  We believe that we can make this method quite
efficient, and possibly generalize it to safely and robustly support
other types of mutable systems.


2.5 Java Infrastructure

We have begun the design of the key Java infrastructure that will support
the design of distributed adaptive signal processing systems.  The core
of this design is a set of classes fashioned after the core of Ptolemy that
support hierarchical graphs as an abstract data structure.  Many
specializations, for example to process networks, are possible.
We plan PN to be the first implemented specialization since it promises
to give us the most benefit from Java threads.

To avoid reimplementing basic data structures, we have decided to use
Doug Lea's Java Collection classes, which are available freely on the
internet.  This decision is based on a careful evaluation of the code
quality and a determination that this is excellent code.

We have had extensive discussions about coding style, naming
conventions, and similar software engineering questions, and have
refined the Tycho development environment to help enforce consistency.


2.6 Multidimensional Signal Processing (contract Option, Questech, Inc)

Negotiations with Thomson continue to make slow but steady progress.
We expect to have a contract finalized within days.

Despite not having officially started this project, we have made some
progress. Brian Evans and Praveen Murthy have found a solution to scheduling
generalized multidimensional synchronous dataflow graphs.  The load
balancing is not only multidimensional but aligned on non-rectangular
grids.  Using the Smith form decomposition of the integer resampling
matrices that represent the non-rectangular grids (lattices), we can
set up the balance equations.  We are in the process of showing that
the Smith Canonical Form is sufficient for generating the balance
equations.  The solution obtained by using the Smith Canonical Form
is rarely optimal in terms of buffering.  We can search through the
space of Smith forms to find the optimal solution with respect to
buffering.


2.7 Technical Improvements to the Software

Kevin Chang separated our Exec class (an interface to external processes)
in Tycho into a graphical and non-graphical portion.  The
non-graphical portion allows scripted operations without user
intervention, while the graphical portion allows for interactive
operations such as compilation and system configuration.

Christopher Hylands wrote a procedure that constructs and displays
the hyperlinks in a set of interconnected HTML pages.  This gives
a graphical map of the relationships between documents.


